{"explain":"In Python, b = a gives two names for one list. In Rust, let b = a with a Vec moves ownership: b now owns the data, and a is unusable. Passing a Vec to a function by value moves it the same way. Copy types such as i32, f64, bool, and char are duplicated on assignment, so both names stay usable. String and Vec<T> are not Copy. clone() makes an explicit duplicate, and its cost depends on the type. Each value has one owner, and when that owner goes out of scope the value is dropped, so memory is freed exactly once without a garbage collector.

Example:
let a = vec![1, 2];
let b = a;
// println!("{:?}", a); // compile error: value moved",

"items":[{"type":"mcq","q":"After let a = vec![1, 2]; let b = a; what is the state of a?","options":["It was moved and can no longer be used","It was copied and still holds its data","It was cloned automatically by the compiler","It is borrowed by b until the scope ends"],"answer":"It was moved and can no longer be used","why":"Vec is not Copy, so assigning it transfers ownership to b and leaves a unusable."},{"type":"mcq","q":"Which of these types is Copy?","options":["String","Vec<u8>","i32","Box<i32>"],"answer":"i32","why":"Integers like i32 are Copy and are duplicated implicitly, while String, Vec, and Box are not."},{"type":"mcq","q":"Given fn take(v: Vec<i32>) {} and let a = vec![1]; take(a); what happens if a is used afterward?","options":["Compile error: value used after move","It prints 1 because a was copied","A runtime panic occurs when a is read","It compiles and a is dropped at runtime"],"answer":"Compile error: value used after move","why":"Passing a non-Copy value by value moves it into the function, so the later use is rejected at compile time."},{"type":"mcq","q":"What best describes clone() in Rust?","options":["An explicit duplicate whose cost depends on the type","An implicit copy that costs the same for every type","A reference that shares the same heap data","A garbage-collected alias of the original value"],"answer":"An explicit duplicate whose cost depends on the type","why":"clone() is an explicit deep duplicate, and its cost varies with what the type holds."},{"type":"recall","q":"In your own words, explain how Rust frees memory without a garbage collector or reference counting, using the idea of ownership.","answer":"Each value has exactly one owner. When that owner goes out of scope, Rust drops the value and frees its memory at that point, deterministically, so no collector needs to scan for unused data.","why":"This ties together single ownership and scope-based dropping, which is the payoff of the move model."},{"type":"recall","q":"Predict whether s is still usable after let s = String::from("hi"); let t = s; and explain why.","answer":"No. String is not Copy, so let t = s moves ownership to t, and using s afterward is a compile error.","why":"This applies the move rule to a heap-owning type, the case most likely to be mistaken for Python-style sharing."}]}
